<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<Pathway xmlns="http://pathvisio.org/GPML/2021" title="traumatin and (Z)-3-hexen-1-yl acetate biosynthesis" organism="cellular organisms" source="PlantCyc" version="20260304101735">
  <Xref identifier="PWY-5410" dataSource="MetaCyc" />
  <Description>&lt;i&gt;Supporting evidence for this pathway in Oryza sativa Japonica Group&lt;/i&gt;: Enzyme(s) that catalyzes one or more of the key reactions in this pathway were predicted in Oryza sativa Japonica Group. &lt;A href=&quot;http://www.plantcyc.org/about/savi-pipeline&quot;&gt;[more info]&lt;/A&gt; &lt;i&gt;Summary from MetaCyc:&lt;/i&gt; The concept of a plant wound hormone was first proposed in 1892 |CITS: [Wiesner92]|. The wound hormone is a compound that stimulates cell division near a trauma site to form a protective callus and to heal the damaged tissue. A quantitative assay for measuring the activity of the wound hormone was developed in 1927, measuring renewal of the parenchymous lining of the seed chambers of immature bean pods |CITS: [Wehnelt27]|. An active compound responsible for this activity was detected in 1937 and named traumatin for the Greek word &lt;i&gt;trauma&lt;/i&gt; (wound) |CITS: [English37][17751236][16653492]|. However, two years later the authors reported that the compound is a dicarboxylic acid and renamed it accordingly as |FRAME: CPD-25932 &quot;traumatic acid&quot;| |CITS: [17791417]|. With the discovery of |FRAME: 12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE| in 1979 it was realized that the latter is the actual natural wound hormone, and that |FRAME: CPD-25932| is formed non-enzymatically by its oxidation in the presence of oxygen |CITS: [16660762]|. |FRAME: 12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE Traumatin| is an oxidation product of polyunsaturated fatty acids in plant tissues, formed by the combined action of lipoxygenases and hydroperoxide lyases. Lipoxygenases (LOX) are ubiquitous enzymes in eukaryotes. Depending on the positional specificities, they are further classified as 9-LOX and 13-LOX, converting poly unsaturated fatty acids to 9-hydroperoxides and 13-hydroperoxides, respectively. In plants, most reported LOXs are 13-LOXs, which is important in the biosynthesis of the plant hormone jasmonic acid (see |FRAME:PWY-735|). The hydroperoxide intermediates can be converted to aldehydes and oxoacids by hydroperoxide lyases (HPL). HPLs are divided into two groups, 9-HPL and 13-HPL, depending on their substrate preferences, either being 9-hydroperoxides, or 13-hydroperoxides. 13-HPL activity has been detected in many plants. The |FRAME: CPLX-1383| encoded by the |FRAME: G-1082| gene was shown to act on products of both LOX9 and LOX13, forming |FRAME: 12-OXO-CIS-9-DODECENOATE|, which is subsequently converted to |FRAME: 12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE| (though the final conversion step has not been described). |FRAME: 12-OXO-CIS-9-DODECENOATE| was shown to be converted to |FRAME: CPD-19335| via |FRAME: RXN-23334| |CITS: [9727603]|, although a non-enzymatic mechanism was also proposed |CITS: [11027649]|. In addition, the aldehydes and alcohols produced by 13-HPL are often responsible for the flavors or odors of plants, for example the odor of cucumbers. One of the products of 13-HPL, |FRAME: CIS-3-HEXENOL|, is converted in a single reaction to |FRAME: CPD-12846|, the major volatile released upon mechanical wounding of the leaves of |FRAME: TAX-3702| |CITS: [17163881]|.</Description>
