    <DataNode elementId="_12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE" textLabel="traumatin" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="INMKWUNQKOWGEZ-VQHVLOKHSA-M" dataSource="InChIKey" />
      <Graphics centerX="364.356201171875" centerY="655.8748779296875" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, nilo-poyanco">The concept of a plant wound hormone was first proposed in 1892 |CITS: [Wiesner92]|. The wound hormone is a compound that stimulates cell division near a trauma site to form a protective callus and to heal the damaged tissue. A quantitative assay for measuring the activity of the wound hormone was developed in 1927, measuring renewal of the parenchymous lining of the seed chambers of immature bean pods |CITS: [Wehnelt27]|. An active compound responsible for this activity was detected in 1937 and named traumatin for the Greek word &lt;i&gt;trauma&lt;/i&gt; (wound) |CITS: [English37][17751236][16653492]|. However, two years later the authors reported that the compound is a dicarboxylic acid and renamed it accordingly as |FRAME: CPD-25932 &quot;traumatic acid&quot;| |CITS: [17791417]|. With the discovery of |FRAME: 12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE| in 1979 it was realized that the latter is the actual natural wound hormone, and that |FRAME: CPD-25932| is formed non-enzymatically by its oxidation in the presence of oxygen |CITS: [16660762]|.</Comment>
      <Property key="UniqueID" value="12-OXO-TRANS-10-DODECENOATE" />
      <Property key="AlternativeId_Zinc" value="ZINC000014619257" />
